# Comparison of regularization strategies

All parameters were selected from the data alone (no access to the true x).
Noise model: white, sigma_hat = 0.0293 (see diagnosis report).

## Per-method results

| method | parameter (selected) | selection criterion | ||y - K x_reg|| | ||x_reg|| | effective dim. |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Tikhonov (method_a) | alpha = 0.01217 | exact LOO-CV (min 9.39e-04) | 0.3998 | 6.932 | 25.9 |
| TSVS (method_b) | r = 26 | GCV (min 9.49e-04) | 0.4053 | 6.798 | 26 |
| CGLS (method_c) | m = 9 | L-curve knee (restricted) | 0.4433 | 3.509 | 3.7 |

## How well each method fits the data
- Residual norms: Tikhonov 0.3998, TSVS 0.4053, CGLS 0.4433.
  All are close to the noise level sigma_hat*sqrt(n) = 0.436; none
  overfits (no residual below the noise floor) and none grossly underfits.

## Parameter selection details and cross-checks (data only)
- Tikhonov: exact LOO-CV has a clean interior minimum at alpha = 0.01217
  (flat valley ~0.003-0.03). Cross-checks: L-curve knee (max distance from the
  chord, log-log) at alpha = 0.01723; noise-floor/Wiener scale
  alpha ~ sigma_hat/tau = 0.02411 (tau = 1.214 from the roughness fit).
  The three data-only scales agree to within a factor ~2,
  with LOO-CV as the primary (directly predictive) criterion.
  Note: GCV is NOT usable for Tikhonov here - it has no interior minimum and
  collapses to alpha -> inf (prefers x = 0), a known pathology when x is rough.
- TSVS: GCV minimum at r = 26, exact LOO-CV minimum at r = 28, and the
  L-curve knee at r = 30 - three independent criteria within 4 of
  each other, all close to the noise-floor effective rank 23.
- CGLS: L-curve knee (restricted to the pre-noise-amplification region
  ||x_m|| <= sqrt(n)*tau ~ 18, beyond which the iterates amplify noise) gives
  m = 9; the classical noise-floor residual stopping rule (first m with
  ||r_m|| <= 1.1*sigma_hat*sqrt(n) = 0.480) gives m = 6.
  The two agree within 3 iteration(s). CGLS LOO-CV is unusable (leverages
  approach 1, 1/(1-c_ii)^2 blows up) and CGLS GCV is degenerate (minimum at m=1).

## Parameter sensitivity
- Tikhonov: alpha x2 -> 38.7%, alpha /2 -> 68.9% relative solution change
  (moderate; a soft filter at the edge of the information limit).
- TSVS: r+2 -> 49.1%, r-2 -> 65.1%, r+5 -> 58.4%, r-5 -> 76.1%
  (hard cutoffs are intrinsically more sensitive to the exact r).
- CGLS: m+5 -> 84.9%, m-5 -> 52.2% (CGLS iterates add whole
  Krylov directions, so the solution is coarse and shifts noticeably).

## Properties of the recovered x
- All three solutions put their coefficient mass in the first ~25 modes
  (the information limit s_i*tau >= sigma); their norms
  (CGLS 3.5 < TSVS 6.8 < Tikhonov 6.9) are far below the
  rough-solution estimate ||x|| ~ 18; this is unavoidable - modes beyond
  i ~ 25 are noise-dominated and no method can recover them.
- CGLS (m = 9) is the most strongly regularized (effective dim ~4):
  CGLS early stopping converges slowly in the x-sense, so few iterations retain
  only the coarsest components.
- Tikhonov and TSVS are close to each other (soft vs hard realization of the
  same spectral cutoff near s ~ 0.018); their solutions agree qualitatively.

## Which result is most trustworthy, and why
Recommended: **Tikhonov with alpha = 0.01217** (method_a).
Justification:
1. Its parameter is selected by exact leave-one-out cross-validation, a directly
   predictive data-only criterion, with a stable interior minimum (flat valley,
   so the choice is not knife-edge).
2. The soft Tikhonov filter s/(s^2+alpha^2) matches the theoretical Wiener/MMSE
   filter form for a rough (iid) x, whose optimal scale is alpha_W = sigma/tau =
   0.024; the selected alpha = 0.01217 lies within a factor ~2.0 of this
   data-estimated optimum (the flat LOO-CV valley spans 0.003-0.03, bracketing it).
3. TSVS with r = 26 (GCV/LOO-CV) is essentially equivalent and independently
   confirms the same effective dimension (~26); it is kept as a robust
   alternative, but hard cutoffs are more sensitive to the exact r.
4. CGLS early stopping is the least trustworthy here: its residual trajectory is
   governed by Krylov convergence (not by singular values), its CV criteria are
   degenerate (LOO-CV diverges, GCV minimum at m=1), only heuristic
   L-curve/noise-floor rules are available, and it regularizes most strongly.

The recommended solution (results/comparison/recommended_solution.csv) is the
Tikhonov solution at alpha = 0.01217.
